{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Not one locust remained in all the border of Egypt.</b> The prayer of Moses at the plague of locusts has endured to this day, that no locust causes damage in all the border of Egypt; and if [locusts] fall in the land of Israel and come and enter the border of Egypt, they do not eat anything at all in all the border of Egypt. And this is a well-known thing." "<b>And behold</b> the words of Moses in his prayer are trustworthy both for the hour and for all generations; and likewise in his utterance that he said, 'only in the river shall they remain' (Exodus 8:24), it has remained there to this day — the water creature called eltimas\"ah (crocodile), and there it grows. And they say that at times it emerges from the waters of the Nile River, where it grows large, and comes out to the bank of the river and swallows whatever it finds, two or three people at once; and no one can prevail against its body with spears and arrows unless they aim to strike its belly. And the physicians have said that it has venom, and one who touches its body harms a person even after death; and it is a species of frog. And so by the power of the words of Moses this creature has remained there, and by the power of his prayer not one locust remained in all the border of Egypt. And thus explained our Master Hananel, of blessed memory, and of this it is said (Psalms 105:2): ״שיחו בכל נפלאותיו״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "The parenthetical קראקאדיל (crocodile) appears to be a marginal gloss identifying eltimas\"ah; rendered as found. Rabbenu Hananel's name resolved from the abbreviated רבינו חננאל ז״ל."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:41.053651992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}